{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v2-1.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v2-1.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"question\": {\n  \"question\": \"v2\",\n  \"about\": \"NVDA\",\n  \"kind\": \"asset\",\n  \"asks\": \"How much in purchase obligations and supply commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as reported in the purchase obligations and contractual commitments disclosures and related notes?\",\n  \"settles_on\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n  \"due\": \"2027-04-01\",\n  \"possible_answers\": [\n   {\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"below 50,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 150,000,000,000 USD to below 200,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"200,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n \"what_the_judge_was_shown\": \"Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much in purchase obligations and supply commitments will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 Form 10-K, as reported in the purchase obligations and contractual commitments disclosures and related notes?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 150,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 150,000,000,000 USD to below 200,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 200,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e49 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Bloomberg (analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg) ; from gnews:df900d43ab873db9b626aa8e 2026-09-22] Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n    Quote: Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n    Quote: For example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e202; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\\n    Quote: Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e557; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e288 [party_statement; Daniel Sparks (fool.com); original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] On the fiscal second-quarter earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect — a decline from the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin.\\n    Quote: Margins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e238; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e295 [fact; Stefon Walters, The Motley Fool; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:b28eba917a011f40661e75dc 2026-09-25] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the foundry that manufactures Nvidia's GPUs, reported second-quarter revenue of $40.2 billion, up 33.7% year over year, and said its margins expanded over the past year: gross margin from 58.6% to 67.7%, operating margin from 49.6% to 60.3%, and net profit margin from 42.7% to 55.6%.\\n    Quote: In the second quarter, TSMC's revenue rose 33.7% year over year to $40.2 billion, but more impressive is how much its margins have expanded. Over the past year, its gross margin rose from 58.6% to 67.7%, operating margin from 49.6% to 60.3%, and net profit margin from 42.7% to 55.6%.\\n- e296 [party_statement; Stefon Walters, The Motley Fool; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:b28eba917a011f40661e75dc 2026-09-25] TSMC guided third-quarter revenue to between $44.6 billion and $45.8 billion, which it said would be 34.7% to 38.4% higher than its $33.1 billion of revenue in the third quarter of 2025.\\n    Quote: It expects Q3 revenue between $44.6 billion and $45.8 billion, which would be 34.7% to 38.4% higher than its $33.1 billion in revenue in Q3 2025.\\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\\n    Quote: It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips\\n- e411 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management said rising memory prices, which rose more than it had expected, will cut gross margin, and expects gross margin to reach its low in fiscal Q4 2027 at 71% to 72%, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Quote: In August 2026, management said rising memory costs will cut NVIDIA’s gross margin, the share of sales kept after production costs. … Management expects gross margin to hit its low in fiscal Q4 2027, at 71% to 72%. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Memory is the cause: management said memory prices rose more than it had expected.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e48; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ), Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek are reportedly lifting 2nm orders by 10% to 20%. That gives TSMC additional visibility as customers prepare next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n- e526 [third_party_view; Marketplace; original source Marketplace 2026-09-28; from gnews:32112c71c9cae87b7ce0d8db 2026-09-28] Marketplace reported, without citing a filing, a named source or a precise figure, that Nvidia has committed hundreds of billions of dollars to the chip supply chain, and that it has also invested in AI companies which then turn around and spend that money on more Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has committed hundreds of billions to the chip supply chain, as well as invested in AI companies that then turn around and spend their money on more Nvidia chips.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) By January 31, 2027, will U.S. authorities authorize Nvidia to sell Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and will Chinese authorities permit those shipments?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s outlook: it excludes China data-center compute revenue, which is closest to rung 3, Still open; it is not a forecast of a formal prohibition. The evidence supports some route to Chinese purchases of the Blackwell-generation RTX PRO 5500, but the reported Chinese intent is u\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026, as shown by their Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 775,000,000,000 USD to below 825,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion, which sits in rung 3. The direct forecast still implies about $800 billion for the five in calendar 2026, within rung 3's $775 billion–below-$825 billion range. Alphabet's $200 billion is a component of the aggregate, not an additional amount; the GPU and capaci\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The available reference is no explicit consensus probability; the reported expectation that MIIT intends to approve some purchase plans, and that some firms were told approval is likely, places the reference at rung 1. I also forecast rung 1: those reports are specific to the firms and product in th\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: There is no market consensus or company guidance for the FY2027 10-K commitment total, so the reference is the ladder's no-reference starting point: $100bn to below $150bn, rung 3. The evidence instead anchors the forecast at $279bn of commitments disclosed at FY2027 Q2, up from $119bn in Q1, and reports that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments while supply remains constrained through FY2028. That makes a year-end disclosure still above $200bn more likely than a fall below the rung-5 boundary. The reported $500bn of HBM locked in is an additional directional signal, but its timeframe and relationship to the $279bn disclosure are unspecified, so it is not taken as the FY2027 10-K figure or added to that amount.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 53,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"AMD Is Up 187% This Year and Nvidia Is Up 22%. Prediction: Nvidia Wins the Next 12 Months.\",\n   \"text\": \"Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) closed Monday, Sept. 21, with a market value above $1 trillion for the first time, making it the fourth U.S. chipmaker to clear that threshold. The stock surged almost 10% that day alone as artificial intelligence (AI) demand boosted chip stocks broadly, taking its 2026 gain to 187% as of this writing.\\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) has had a solid year by most measures, up about 22% in 2026. Its stock has not closed at a record since mid-May, though. That gap is striking given that Nvidia is more than five times AMD's size and, currently, the faster-growing of the two.\\nBoth stocks now come with high expectations. The difference, I'd say, is in how much each price requires the company to prove over the next year. Here's why I expect Nvidia stock to beat AMD over the next 12 months.\\nNvidia's fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26) was another exceptional one. Revenue grew 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, an acceleration from the previous quarter, and the data center business contributed all but about $7 billion of the total. Profit kept up. Net income rose 126% year over year, to $59.7 billion. And management guided for another step up in this quarter (fiscal Q3): $108.0 billion of revenue.\\nOn the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further.\\n\\\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\\\" Kress said. \\\"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\\nPut another way, the cap on next year's growth is how many chips Nvidia can get made, not how many customers want them. And CEO Jensen Huang told analysts demand without that constraint \\\"would be a lot higher.\\\"\\nAt Monday's close of $227.38, shares trade at about 15 times what the company is expected to earn in fiscal 2028, the year that outlook applies to. That price, in my view, does not require believing much more than what management has already put on the record.\\nAMD's rally has real results behind it. Revenue for the second quarter of 2026 hit a record $11.5 billion, up 50% from a year ago.\\nAnd the data center segment, which sells EPYC server processors and Instinct graphics processing units into the same AI build-out Nvidia serves, more than doubled year over year, totaling $6.7 billion (58% of the company's revenue).\\nManagement expects more from here. Guidance for the third quarter of 2026 is around $13 billion, which implies about 41% year-over-year growth. And CEO Lisa Su told analysts on the company's August earnings call that the bigger move happens next year.\\n\\\"[W]e now expect data center segment revenue to more than double year-over-year in 2027,\\\" Su said.\\nThe problem is what that outlook now costs. At Monday's closing price of $615.52, the stock trades at about 40 times expected 2027 earnings -- and those estimates already call for earnings roughly doubling next year.\\nThe run has been quick, too. Shares climbed about 22% in the four trading days through Monday's close, roughly equaling what Nvidia's stock has gained all year.\\nWhich stock wins the next 12 months?\\nBuying AMD today means paying about 40 times 2027's projected earnings for a plan management has set out but not yet executed. It also means betting the market will pay up once more a year from now for whatever 2028 is expected to hold. After a 187% run, that's a lot of ongoing enthusiasm to rely on.\\nBuying Nvidia, at about 15 times the consensus estimate for fiscal 2028, means paying for growth management describes as limited only by supply. If the company just delivers the year it's guided for, today's price doesn't look demanding.\\nSure, momentum could keep carrying AMD. Su said customer pull for the company's new Helios AI systems is \\\"very strong and tracking ahead of our initial forecast,\\\" and estimates could keep rising. Of course, a sharp decline in AI spending would take both stocks down, expensive or not -- and the more expensive one arguably has further to fall.\\nIn the end, I expect Nvidia stock to do better than AMD over the next 12 months. Both companies are executing about as well as investors could ask. But Nvidia's price leaves much more room for ordinary disappointment.\\nBetween the two, Nvidia is the one I'd buy today. If AMD's data center revenue doubles again in 2027 while the stock cools down, I'd take another look at the comparison.\\nThe latest $1 trillion chipmaker now costs about 40 times its expected 2027 earnings. Its bigger, faster-growing competitor costs about 15 times its own.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 23, 2026 at 7:58PM EST\\nAMD costs about 40 times its 2027 consensus earnings estimate, and Nvidia costs about 15 times its fiscal 2028 estimate.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 92,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA's $279B Supply Commitments: Can These Secure Its Growth Runway?\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 99,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"semafor.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang dismisses AI fears as ‘distraction’\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang dismissed fears over AI’s existential threats as a “distraction” that’s “ not grounded on science ,” as the heads of OpenAI and Anthropic warned the UN of AI’s risks to humanity.\\nIf labs insist their products can’t be controlled then “we have to shut the labs down,” Huang argued on The Ezra Klein Show , remarks that clashed with warnings from experts and tech leaders over the catastrophic consequences of mishandling AI.\\nCritics charged Huang with failing to recognize the gravity of the moment; one called his comments “ confused… [and] simplistic .”\\nHuang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year, has emerged as one of the AI boom’s chief global boosters, aligning with US President Donald Trump.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 120,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"marketbeat.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA’s Next AI Chip Ramp Could Open the Door to Another Major Stock Move\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA's accelerating AI chip production reflects continued demand for computing capacity from hyperscalers, enterprises and sovereign customers.\\nWall Street price targets continue to rise as NVIDIA expands its AI ecosystem and ramps its next generation of Vera Rubin systems.\\nNVIDIA's growth also creates opportunities for server manufacturers and other companies supplying the infrastructure needed to deploy AI at scale.\\nNVIDIA's (NASDAQ: NVDA) forecast to double its chip volume next year strengthens an already bullish growth outlook for the company and its stock. Rapid computing expansion, underpinned by sovereigns and enterprises, coupled with product launches, creates an ecosystem flywheel and virtuous cycle that could keep NVIDIA growing at a solid pace well into the future.\\nFor NVIDIA investors, this means the potential for persistent upgrades to revenue and earnings targets, sentiment, and price targets—a bullish cycle for the stock price outlook. As it stands, 55 analysts cover this stock; new coverage is still emerging, and only one Hold rating. Price targets are the operative factor here, with the consensus up more than 50% over the trailing 12 months and forecasting about 45% upside relative to late-September price action. A 50% upside isn't a triple-digit gain, but it is a step in the right direction and the trend matters. Summer revisions push the high end into the $515 range, representing more than 100% upside from the critical resistance point—and it could still prove to be a low-ball estimate.\\n→ Oil May Be Stronger Than It Looks—And Diamondback Is on Sale\\nNVIDIA's Valuation Is Disconnected From AI's Reality\\nNVIDIA's latest growth outlook also reinforces the case that it could retain its dominant position in AI. Advanced Micro Devices is a solid up-and-comer, positioned for its own explosive growth cycle, but NVIDIA remains the dominant player, particularly for the most advanced workloads. With this in mind, the valuation gains suggested by the price multiples become clearer.\\nNVIDIA's price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio aligns with the S&P 500 average as of late September, but fails to price in the company's dominance. Trading at about 24x current-year earnings, the market puts no premium on it, and no growth expectation is priced in. Looking forward, the discount deepens to only 10x earnings within two years, and the 10-year forecast suggests this stock trades at pennies on the dollar, about 5x earnings, revealing a structural disconnection with the fundamental outlook.\\nIf NVIDIA continues to fire on all cylinders and grows into or exceeds its earnings forecast, its stock price could rise by several hundred percentage points over the next few years, potentially as much as 500%.\\nThe trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM). That rollout could unlock another wave of data center construction. Many investors get it wrong that all the data centers are being built now; many, including much of Oracle's (NASDAQ: ORCL) backlog, are back-ended and waiting on the next-gen performance Vera Rubin promises.\\nThe companies benefiting most from NVIDIA's sales explosion include the OEMs that put NVIDIA's hardware and software into commercialized packages. While one GPU is powerful, AI requires servers, server clusters, rack clusters, and buildings filled with racks to work. Companies deploying NVIDIA technology at scale include Dell Technologies (NYSE: DELL), Hewlett Packard Enterprise (NYSE: HPE), Super Micro Computer (NASDAQ: SMCI), and Cisco Systems (NASDAQ: CSCO), all of which reflect strength in their numbers.\\nNVIDIA's biggest risk is credit-related. It's not that NVIDIA's credit quality is eroding so much as the amount of future sales it is self-financing. NVIDIA customers could undercut the revenue outlook with capital expenditure (CapEx) reductions, but this is unlikely, given the need for capacity and Wall Street insiders' moves to securitize GPUs. Securitizing GPUs is a bigger move than some give credit for and is likely to unlock additional funding as initial investments begin paying off.\\nThe offset is slowing the advance of frontier modeling. Slowing AI's advance removes the industry's biggest hurdle: cash flow. It also enables monetization of existing AI infrastructure, strengthens balance sheets, and de-risks the outlook for NVIDIA's future sales.\\nThe market gets it wrong when it assumes that NVIDIA's periodic growth slowdowns signal deteriorating demand. While supply bottlenecks and product transitions impact quarterly results, underlying demand remains robust, and this is all without the impact of China. Meanwhile, the company is selling its chips within full systems, deepening and widening its moat with vendor lock-in, while advancing AI at all levels of the stack. That enables incredible pricing power, stable margins, and ample cash flow.\\nCash flow is critical, as NVIDIA is not only profitable but also self-funding growth, investing in its supply chain, future capacity, and new technology. Equally important, NVIDIA also has capacity for capital return, including dividends and share buybacks, and is on pace to accelerate them over the coming year.\\nThe article \\\" NVIDIA's Next AI Chip Ramp Could Open the Door to Another Major Stock Move \\\" was originally published by MarketBeat.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 121,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 140,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"A $1,000 Investment Split Between Alphabet and Nvidia Will Be Worth This Much by 2030\",\n   \"text\": \"Alphabet ( GOOG -0.77% ) ( GOOGL -0.61% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) are two of the biggest companies involved in the AI race. Nvidia is the world's largest company, while Alphabet is the third-largest behind Apple , which isn't pursuing AI technology as aggressively as Alphabet.\\nBoth of these companies have an incredibly bright future, and I think they will make investors a lot of money from now through 2030. How much will a $1,000 investment split between the two be worth? Let's take a look.\\nAlphabet and Nvidia's success will look different\\nAlphabet and Nvidia are both competitors and partners.\\nNvidia's approach to AI is a bit more straightforward than Alphabet's, as it's focused on selling as many computing units as possible to power AI workloads. Its product lineup is centered around the graphics processing unit (GPU) , which excels at nearly every task you can throw at it. GPUs are used by every company in the AI race (including Alphabet) and will be used for the foreseeable future.\\nHowever, they aren't always the right tool for the job. Sometimes a GPU can go its whole lifespan running the same workload. This is a waste of its capabilities, and it could be replaced by a computing unit purpose-built for that workload. Alphabet has done just that by designing and outsourcing the fabrication of its tensor processing unit (TPU) to Broadcom .\\nAlphabet's TPUs are incredibly popular, and it's even selling them to others because they represent an upgrade over a GPU when less flexibility is needed. This could eat into Nvidia's business over the next few years, but with Nvidia expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, I'd say it's doing just fine.\\nAlphabet also has other AI investments. It has integrated AI into Google Search, its primary cash cow. Alphabet also has its own family of large language models integrated into various AI products, which could be a growing source of revenue. Perhaps its biggest growth division will be Google Cloud, its cloud computing division.\\nCloud computing is a major beneficiary of the AI build-out. Many AI businesses lack the computing power to run their operations, so they rent it from a hyperscaler like Alphabet. During the second quarter, this division grew at a remarkable 82% pace, and with Alphabet spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, the growth rate will likely stay elevated for a long time.\\nBoth Alphabet and Nvidia have grown their business prospects, but where will that place their stocks by 2030?\\nNvidia will likely provide greater returns\\nUnderstanding what 2030 will be like for these two will be difficult, but I think there's enough information to get us there. Nvidia estimates global data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. Right now, the big five AI hyperscalers are spending around $800 billion. That's about a fourfold increase in total market opportunity over the next few years. If Nvidia can quadruple its revenue, that would likely result in the stock following suit, because it trades at a reasonable price tag of 28 times earnings.\\nAlphabet is a bit trickier. I think that, with the investments it's making, it could grow revenue and profits at a 25% compound annual growth rate (CAGR), given that it grew at 24% in the second quarter. A 25% CAGR from now until the end of 2030 equates to a 158% growth rate.\\nThat indicates that Alphabet's stock will more than double . So, a $1,000 investment split between these two stocks, using these projections, indicates a final value of over $3,000. That would be an incredible gain in just a bit over four years, making these two stocks no-brainer buys right now.\\nBy Keithen Drury – Sep 26, 2026 at 10:15PM EST\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 143,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Whose AI Bull Case is Better?\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) is at the top of the AI computing food chain, holding the largest market share by far and being quite a bit larger than most of its competitors. However, Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) is taking a different approach to the AI computing world and also looks like a strong contender, especially as its custom AI chips gain popularity.\\nBoth of these make for fantastic AI investments and are easily in my top five best stocks to buy now, but which one has the better bull case? Let's take a look.\\nNvidia is quite a bit larger than Broadcom\\nBroadcom is the new kid on the block, while Nvidia is the established giant. Nvidia makes broad-purpose computing units, known as GPUs . GPUs can handle a wide variety of workloads and are incredibly powerful. GPUs have been used for nearly every computing application that requires a lot of computing power, and AI has been no different.\\nNvidia's products were by far and away better than everyone else's at the start of the AI race, and that really hasn't changed. Nvidia has become synonymous with data centers and computing, and there's a good reason for that.\\nHowever, GPUs are not always the best tools for the job. While GPUs can run nearly every workload incredibly well, they aren't optimized for everything. In fact, some GPUs work their whole computing lifespan on one workload, wasting a lot of their capabilities. That's where an ASIC, application-specific integrated circuit, makes a lot of sense. ASICs have been around for a while, and Broadcom is bringing that concept to the AI world to make computing units that are purpose-built for their end user.\\nWhile these computing units can't compete with the GPU in terms of flexibility, they can outperform GPUs in a specific task at a lower price point.\\nThe question isn't if the future holds GPUs or custom AI chips; it's what kind of mix the AI hyperscalers will be buying. If the projections from these two are to be believed, then it's clear that GPUs will still hold a fair bit of market share moving forward.\\nBroadcom is gaining ground, but not fast enough\\nBroadcom issued bold guidance for future growth, but it has already exceeded previously established figures, so it has a track record of outperforming expectations. For fiscal year 2027, Broadcom expects to generate $115 billion in AI semiconductor revenue. In FY 2028, that figure doubles to $230 billion. Those are great growth figures, and any Broadcom investor is excited about seeing those numbers. However, Nvidia has already exceeded those figures by a long shot.\\nDuring the second quarter, Nvidia generated $96.2 billion in revenue. For Q3, it expects $108 billion. More than 90% of Nvidia's revenue comes from its data center division, so Nvidia's quarterly revenue figures nearly exceed what Broadcom expects to generate from AI semiconductors during FY 2027. That's a sizable gap, but with Broadcom doubling its revenue again in FY 2028, can Broadcom expect to gain ground?\\nIt may gain some .\\nFor FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%. While this is slower than Broadcom's AI semiconductor growth rate, it's still very fast.\\nSo, which computing unit will reign supreme? It's clear that Broadcom's custom AI chips are growing faster and taking some market share, but Nvidia's GPU-based products are still dominant and are rapidly growing as well. I don't think Nvidia investors have anything to worry about with Broadcom, and each looks like a solid buy.\\nHowever, between the two, I'm giving the nod to Nvidia . It's hard to argue with the universal nature of its GPUs, where Broadcom is one slip-up away from losing a major client. However, I'd also buy the argument that Broadcom is the better pick due to its faster growth. Regardless of which stock you choose (you don't have to pick only one), I think you'll be satisfied with the returns these two provide over the next few years.\\nBy Keithen Drury – Sep 26, 2026 at 4:02AM EST\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 147,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"publisher\": \"currently.att.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Whose AI Bull Case is Better?\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Whose AI Bull Case is Better?\\nNvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) is at the top of the AI computing food chain, holding the largest market share by far and being quite a bit larger than most of its competitors. However, Broadcom (NASDAQ: AVGO) is taking a different approach to the AI computing world and also looks like a strong contender, especially as its custom AI chips gain popularity.\\nBoth of these make for fantastic AI investments and are easily in my top five best stocks to buy now, but which one has the better bull case? Let's take a look.\\nMissed AI's \\\"Act 1\\\"? Act 2 Could Be 15x Bigger. Most investors think they missed the AI boat because they didn't buy Nvidia in 2005. But according to our analysts, we're only at the end of \\\"Act 1\\\"—the R&D phase. \\\"Act 2\\\" is the global rollout. Continue »\\nNvidia is quite a bit larger than Broadcom\\nBroadcom is the new kid on the block, while Nvidia is the established giant. Nvidia makes broad-purpose computing units, known as GPUs . GPUs can handle a wide variety of workloads and are incredibly powerful. GPUs have been used for nearly every computing application that requires a lot of computing power, and AI has been no different.\\nNvidia's products were by far and away better than everyone else's at the start of the AI race, and that really hasn't changed. Nvidia has become synonymous with data centers and computing, and there's a good reason for that.\\nHowever, GPUs are not always the best tools for the job. While GPUs can run nearly every workload incredibly well, they aren't optimized for everything. In fact, some GPUs work their whole computing lifespan on one workload, wasting a lot of their capabilities. That's where an ASIC, application-specific integrated circuit, makes a lot of sense. ASICs have been around for a while, and Broadcom is bringing that concept to the AI world to make computing units that are purpose-built for their end user.\\nWhile these computing units can't compete with the GPU in terms of flexibility, they can outperform GPUs in a specific task at a lower price point.\\nThe question isn't if the future holds GPUs or custom AI chips; it's what kind of mix the AI hyperscalers will be buying. If the projections from these two are to be believed, then it's clear that GPUs will still hold a fair bit of market share moving forward.\\nBroadcom is gaining ground, but not fast enough\\nBroadcom issued bold guidance for future growth, but it has already exceeded previously established figures, so it has a track record of outperforming expectations. For fiscal year 2027, Broadcom expects to generate $115 billion in AI semiconductor revenue. In FY 2028, that figure doubles to $230 billion. Those are great growth figures, and any Broadcom investor is excited about seeing those numbers. However, Nvidia has already exceeded those figures by a long shot.\\nDuring the second quarter, Nvidia generated $96.2 billion in revenue. For Q3, it expects $108 billion. More than 90% of Nvidia's revenue comes from its data center division, so Nvidia's quarterly revenue figures nearly exceed what Broadcom expects to generate from AI semiconductors during FY 2027. That's a sizable gap, but with Broadcom doubling its revenue again in FY 2028, can Broadcom expect to gain ground?\\nIt may gain some .\\nFor FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%. While this is slower than Broadcom's AI semiconductor growth rate, it's still very fast.\\nSo, which computing unit will reign supreme? It's clear that Broadcom's custom AI chips are growing faster and taking some market share, but Nvidia's GPU-based products are still dominant and are rapidly growing as well. I don't think Nvidia investors have anything to worry about with Broadcom, and each looks like a solid buy.\\nHowever, between the two, I'm giving the nod to Nvidia . It's hard to argue with the universal nature of its GPUs, where Broadcom is one slip-up away from losing a major client. However, I'd also buy the argument that Broadcom is the better pick due to its faster growth. Regardless of which stock you choose (you don't have to pick only one), I think you'll be satisfied with the returns these two provide over the next few years.\\nShould you buy stock in Nvidia right now?\\nBefore you buy stock in Nvidia, consider this:\\nConsider when Netflix made this list on December 17, 2004... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation, you'd have $386,781 !* Or when Nvidia made this list on April 15, 2005... if you invested $1,000 at the time of our recommendation, you'd have $1,379,943 !*\\nNvidia vs. Broadcom: Whose AI Bull Case is Better? was originally published by The Motley Fool\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 158,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"3 Stocks to Load Up on Before 2026 Is Over\",\n   \"text\": \"As 2026 draws to the final quarter, investors should direct some of their attention to what may work in 2027.\\nI think 2027 will be another year of dominance by artificial intelligence stocks (surprise, surprise). While some investors may be growing weary of the AI trade, I think several factors will boost this industry and make some of the biggest winners over the past few years look like attractive investments.\\nSo, what three stocks do I think are the best to buy before the year is over? I think it's easily Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , and Micron ( MU +4.25% ) .\\nAI is expected to boom again in 2027\\nOne of the reasons I think AI stocks will see success in 2027 is the result of some IPOs that haven't happened. OpenAI and Anthropic are two of the biggest names in the AI model space, and make the ChatGPT and Claude AI models. These two companies are expected to go public sometime in 2026, and could have a valuation of $2 trillion each attached to them. That will be some major new additions to the stock market, but I think it will just highlight the need for more computing power.\\nThat's where these three come in.\\nNvidia is the primary AI computing unit provider, and nearly every AI firm uses Nvidia GPUs in some way. Nvidia is the industry standard computing unit for a reason, and it expects incredible growth in 2027 as a company and for the industry as a whole.\\nNext year, its management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace. If you've followed Nvidia throughout the AI race, you know that management often sandbags its guidance, so the real growth rate is likely higher than this. As for a large industry outlook, Nvidia expects the big five AI hyper-scalers to spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion next year.\\nThat leaves plenty of room for growth for Nvidia , but also its competitors.\\nBroadcom is a rising star in the AI world\\nNvidia makes broad-purpose computing chips, while Broadcom's are far more specialized. Broadcom has partnered with several AI firms, including OpenAI and Anthropic, to design a computing unit that is specifically tailored for their workloads. This makes a cheaper and more streamlined computing process, but it must be used for its optimized workload. Outside of that, the computing unit is useless. This makes them perfect for some applications, but not all.\\nThere's plenty of room for Nvidia and Broadcom to thrive in the AI race. Broadcom is expected to grow quicker than Nvidia, as its AI semiconductor revenue is more than doubling to $115 billion next year.\\nThat's a solid growth rate, and it makes Broadcom a no-brainer investment right now .\\nNvidia and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without memory chips, and currently, there's a huge shortage. Micron is one of the biggest memory chip providers in this space and is benefiting from soaring prices.\\nWhile Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028. Micron also doesn't expect the \\\"tightness\\\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028, which leaves plenty of time for the stock to boom.\\nEven after the tightness is alleviated, memory chips will still be in high demand. This will extend Micron's growth cycle for many years beyond 2028, especially if AI hyperscalers continue spending hundreds of billions of dollars on data centers through 2030 and later.\\nAll three of these stocks are slated to cash in on the AI buildout. With the AI buildout intensifying in 2027, they make for no-brainer buys now to position your portfolio wisely heading into the new year.\\nBy Keithen Drury – Sep 26, 2026 at 3:45AM EST\"\n  }\n ],\n \"items\": [\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e114\",\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e115\",\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"The article reports that NVIDIA's days sales outstanding (DSO) is extending to 60 days, listing it among the company's real risks.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e116\",\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA (Jensen Huang)\",\n   \"summary\": \"Jensen Huang told investors that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, and the article states fiscal 2028 growth is pegged at approximately 70%.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e130\",\n   \"article\": 53,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Nvidia (Colette Kress, CFO)\",\n   \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's earnings call in late August 2026, chief financial officer Colette Kress guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of approximately 70%, characterising the outlook as supply-constrained.\",\n   \"quote\": \"On the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further. … \\\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\\\" Kress said. \\\"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e205\",\n   \"article\": 92,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, Zacks reports, while noting that supply constraints could limit how quickly the company meets demand.\",\n   \"quote\": \"NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e235\",\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, the first time it has guided a full year ahead; its CEO said demand is much greater than 70% and that supply, not demand, holds the guide at that level.\",\n   \"quote\": \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e238\",\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e240\",\n   \"article\": 99,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said NVIDIA would double its chip sales next year, according to Semafor’s report.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Huang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e282\",\n   \"article\": 120,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA forecasts that it will double its AI chip volume next year (2027, the year after this 25 September 2026 article's publication), a forecast MarketBeat says strengthens the company's already bullish growth outlook for the company and its stock.\",\n   \"quote\": \"NVIDIA's (NASDAQ: NVDA) forecast to double its chip volume next year strengthens an already bullish growth outlook for the company and its stock.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e286\",\n   \"article\": 121,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n   \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand.\",\n   \"quote\": \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e327\",\n   \"article\": 140,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Keithen Drury (fool.com)\",\n   \"summary\": \"The fool.com author writes that Nvidia is expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, presented as a reason that Alphabet's TPU sales will not derail Nvidia; no source for the expectation is named.\",\n   \"quote\": \"This could eat into Nvidia's business over the next few years, but with Nvidia expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, I'd say it's doing just fine.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e339\",\n   \"article\": 143,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Nvidia\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia estimates that its revenue growth for fiscal 2027 will be around 70%, according to Fool.com contributor Keithen Drury.\",\n   \"quote\": \"For FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e351\",\n   \"article\": 147,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"Nvidia estimates its revenue growth for fiscal year 2027 will be around 70%, a rate the article calls slower than Broadcom's AI semiconductor growth, according to The Motley Fool.\",\n   \"quote\": \"For FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%. While this is slower than Broadcom's AI semiconductor growth rate, it's still very fast.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e366\",\n   \"article\": 158,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"summary\": \"According to The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury, NVIDIA's management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace in 2027 (\\\"next year\\\"), and Drury adds that management often sandbags its guidance so the real growth rate is likely higher.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Next year, its management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace. If you've followed Nvidia throughout the AI race, you know that management often sandbags its guidance, so the real growth rate is likely higher than this.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v2:e368\",\n   \"article\": 158,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Micron\",\n   \"summary\": \"Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\",\n   \"quote\": \"While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\"\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v2-1.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in [46,53,92,98,99,120,121,140]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v2-1.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] in [46,53,92,98,99,120,121,140]]'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 46 NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\nARTICLE 53 Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) closed Monday, Sept. 21, with a market value above $1 trillion for the first time, making it the fourth U.S. chipmaker to clear that threshold. The stock surged almost 10% that day alone as artificial intelligence (AI) demand boosted chip stocks broadly, taking its 2026 gain to 187% as of this writing.\nNvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) has had a solid year by most measures, up about 22% in 2026. Its stock has not closed at a record since mid-May, though. That gap is striking given that Nvidia is more than five times AMD's size and, currently, the faster-growing of the two.\nBoth stocks now come with high expectations. The difference, I'd say, is in how much each price requires the company to prove over the next year. Here's why I expect Nvidia stock to beat AMD over the next 12 months.\nNvidia's fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26) was another exceptional one. Revenue grew 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, an acceleration from the previous quarter, and the data center business contributed all but about $7 billion of the total. Profit kept up. Net income rose 126% year over year, to $59.7 billion. And management guided for another step up in this quarter (fiscal Q3): $108.0 billion of revenue.\nOn the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further.\n\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\" Kress said. \"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\"\nPut another way, the cap on next year's growth is how many chips Nvidia can get made, not how many customers want them. And CEO Jensen Huang told analysts demand without that constraint \"would be a lot higher.\"\nAt Monday's close of $227.38, shares trade at about 15 times what the company is expected to earn in fiscal 2028, the year that outlook applies to. That price, in my view, does not require believing much more than what management has already put on the record.\nAMD's rally has real results behind it. Revenue for the second quarter of 2026 hit a record $11.5 billion, up 50% from a year ago.\nAnd the data center segment, which sells EPYC server processors and Instinct graphics processing units into the same AI build-out Nvidia serves, more than doubled year over year, totaling $6.7 billion (58% of the company's revenue).\nManagement expects more from here. Guidance for the third quarter of 2026 is around $13 billion, which implies about 41% year-over-year growth. And CEO Lisa Su told analysts on the company's August earnings call that the bigger move happens next year.\n\"[W]e now expect data center segment revenue to more than double year-over-year in 2027,\" Su said.\nThe problem is what that outlook now costs. At Monday's closing price of $615.52, the stock trades at about 40 times expected 2027 earnings -- and those estimates already call for earnings roughly doubling next year.\nThe run has been quick, too. Shares climbed about 22% in the four trading days through Monday's close, roughly equaling what Nvidia's stock has gained all year.\nWhich stock wins the next 12 months?\nBuying AMD today means paying about 40 times 2027's projected earnings for a plan management has set out but not yet executed. It also means betting the market will pay up once more a year from now for whatever 2028 is expected to hold. After a 187% run, that's a lot of ongoing enthusiasm to rely on.\nBuying Nvidia, at about 15 times the consensus estimate for fiscal 2028, means paying for growth management describes as limited only by supply. If the company just delivers the year it's guided for, today's price doesn't look demanding.\nSure, momentum could keep carrying AMD. Su said customer pull for the company's new Helios AI systems is \"very strong and tracking ahead of our initial forecast,\" and estimates could keep rising. Of course, a sharp decline in AI spending would take both stocks down, expensive or not -- and the more expensive one arguably has further to fall.\nIn the end, I expect Nvidia stock to do better than AMD over the next 12 months. Both companies are executing about as well as investors could ask. But Nvidia's price leaves much more room for ordinary disappointment.\nBetween the two, Nvidia is the one I'd buy today. If AMD's data center revenue doubles again in 2027 while the stock cools down, I'd take another look at the comparison.\nThe latest $1 trillion chipmaker now costs about 40 times its expected 2027 earnings. Its bigger, faster-growing competitor costs about 15 times its own.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 23, 2026 at 7:58PM EST\nAMD costs about 40 times its 2027 consensus earnings estimate, and Nvidia costs about 15 times its fiscal 2028 estimate.\nARTICLE 92 NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\nARTICLE 98 NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\nARTICLE 99 Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang dismissed fears over AI’s existential threats as a “distraction” that’s “ not grounded on science ,” as the heads of OpenAI and Anthropic warned the UN of AI’s risks to humanity.\nIf labs insist their products can’t be controlled then “we have to shut the labs down,” Huang argued on The Ezra Klein Show , remarks that clashed with warnings from experts and tech leaders over the catastrophic consequences of mishandling AI.\nCritics charged Huang with failing to recognize the gravity of the moment; one called his comments “ confused… [and] simplistic .”\nHuang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year, has emerged as one of the AI boom’s chief global boosters, aligning with US President Donald Trump.\nARTICLE 120 NVIDIA's accelerating AI chip production reflects continued demand for computing capacity from hyperscalers, enterprises and sovereign customers.\nWall Street price targets continue to rise as NVIDIA expands its AI ecosystem and ramps its next generation of Vera Rubin systems.\nNVIDIA's growth also creates opportunities for server manufacturers and other companies supplying the infrastructure needed to deploy AI at scale.\nNVIDIA's (NASDAQ: NVDA) forecast to double its chip volume next year strengthens an already bullish growth outlook for the company and its stock. Rapid computing expansion, underpinned by sovereigns and enterprises, coupled with product launches, creates an ecosystem flywheel and virtuous cycle that could keep NVIDIA growing at a solid pace well into the future.\nFor NVIDIA investors, this means the potential for persistent upgrades to revenue and earnings targets, sentiment, and price targets—a bullish cycle for the stock price outlook. As it stands, 55 analysts cover this stock; new coverage is still emerging, and only one Hold rating. Price targets are the operative factor here, with the consensus up more than 50% over the trailing 12 months and forecasting about 45% upside relative to late-September price action. A 50% upside isn't a triple-digit gain, but it is a step in the right direction and the trend matters. Summer revisions push the high end into the $515 range, representing more than 100% upside from the critical resistance point—and it could still prove to be a low-ball estimate.\n→ Oil May Be Stronger Than It Looks—And Diamondback Is on Sale\nNVIDIA's Valuation Is Disconnected From AI's Reality\nNVIDIA's latest growth outlook also reinforces the case that it could retain its dominant position in AI. Advanced Micro Devices is a solid up-and-comer, positioned for its own explosive growth cycle, but NVIDIA remains the dominant player, particularly for the most advanced workloads. With this in mind, the valuation gains suggested by the price multiples become clearer.\nNVIDIA's price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio aligns with the S&P 500 average as of late September, but fails to price in the company's dominance. Trading at about 24x current-year earnings, the market puts no premium on it, and no growth expectation is priced in. Looking forward, the discount deepens to only 10x earnings within two years, and the 10-year forecast suggests this stock trades at pennies on the dollar, about 5x earnings, revealing a structural disconnection with the fundamental outlook.\nIf NVIDIA continues to fire on all cylinders and grows into or exceeds its earnings forecast, its stock price could rise by several hundred percentage points over the next few years, potentially as much as 500%.\nThe trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM). That rollout could unlock another wave of data center construction. Many investors get it wrong that all the data centers are being built now; many, including much of Oracle's (NASDAQ: ORCL) backlog, are back-ended and waiting on the next-gen performance Vera Rubin promises.\nThe companies benefiting most from NVIDIA's sales explosion include the OEMs that put NVIDIA's hardware and software into commercialized packages. While one GPU is powerful, AI requires servers, server clusters, rack clusters, and buildings filled with racks to work. Companies deploying NVIDIA technology at scale include Dell Technologies (NYSE: DELL), Hewlett Packard Enterprise (NYSE: HPE), Super Micro Computer (NASDAQ: SMCI), and Cisco Systems (NASDAQ: CSCO), all of which reflect strength in their numbers.\nNVIDIA's biggest risk is credit-related. It's not that NVIDIA's credit quality is eroding so much as the amount of future sales it is self-financing. NVIDIA customers could undercut the revenue outlook with capital expenditure (CapEx) reductions, but this is unlikely, given the need for capacity and Wall Street insiders' moves to securitize GPUs. Securitizing GPUs is a bigger move than some give credit for and is likely to unlock additional funding as initial investments begin paying off.\nThe offset is slowing the advance of frontier modeling. Slowing AI's advance removes the industry's biggest hurdle: cash flow. It also enables monetization of existing AI infrastructure, strengthens balance sheets, and de-risks the outlook for NVIDIA's future sales.\nThe market gets it wrong when it assumes that NVIDIA's periodic growth slowdowns signal deteriorating demand. While supply bottlenecks and product transitions impact quarterly results, underlying demand remains robust, and this is all without the impact of China. Meanwhile, the company is selling its chips within full systems, deepening and widening its moat with vendor lock-in, while advancing AI at all levels of the stack. That enables incredible pricing power, stable margins, and ample cash flow.\nCash flow is critical, as NVIDIA is not only profitable but also self-funding growth, investing in its supply chain, future capacity, and new technology. Equally important, NVIDIA also has capacity for capital return, including dividends and share buybacks, and is on pace to accelerate them over the coming year.\nThe article \" NVIDIA's Next AI Chip Ramp Could Open the Door to Another Major Stock Move \" was originally published by MarketBeat.\nARTICLE 121 Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\nARTICLE 140 Alphabet ( GOOG -0.77% ) ( GOOGL -0.61% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) are two of the biggest companies involved in the AI race. Nvidia is the world's largest company, while Alphabet is the third-largest behind Apple , which isn't pursuing AI technology as aggressively as Alphabet.\nBoth of these companies have an incredibly bright future, and I think they will make investors a lot of money from now through 2030. How much will a $1,000 investment split between the two be worth? Let's take a look.\nAlphabet and Nvidia's success will look different\nAlphabet and Nvidia are both competitors and partners.\nNvidia's approach to AI is a bit more straightforward than Alphabet's, as it's focused on selling as many computing units as possible to power AI workloads. Its product lineup is centered around the graphics processing unit (GPU) , which excels at nearly every task you can throw at it. GPUs are used by every company in the AI race (including Alphabet) and will be used for the foreseeable future.\nHowever, they aren't always the right tool for the job. Sometimes a GPU can go its whole lifespan running the same workload. This is a waste of its capabilities, and it could be replaced by a computing unit purpose-built for that workload. Alphabet has done just that by designing and outsourcing the fabrication of its tensor processing unit (TPU) to Broadcom .\nAlphabet's TPUs are incredibly popular, and it's even selling them to others because they represent an upgrade over a GPU when less flexibility is needed. This could eat into Nvidia's business over the next few years, but with Nvidia expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, I'd say it's doing just fine.\nAlphabet also has other AI investments. It has integrated AI into Google Search, its primary cash cow. Alphabet also has its own family of large language models integrated into various AI products, which could be a growing source of revenue. Perhaps its biggest growth division will be Google Cloud, its cloud computing division.\nCloud computing is a major beneficiary of the AI build-out. Many AI businesses lack the computing power to run their operations, so they rent it from a hyperscaler like Alphabet. During the second quarter, this division grew at a remarkable 82% pace, and with Alphabet spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, the growth rate will likely stay elevated for a long time.\nBoth Alphabet and Nvidia have grown their business prospects, but where will that place their stocks by 2030?\nNvidia will likely provide greater returns\nUnderstanding what 2030 will be like for these two will be difficult, but I think there's enough information to get us there. Nvidia estimates global data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. Right now, the big five AI hyperscalers are spending around $800 billion. That's about a fourfold increase in total market opportunity over the next few years. If Nvidia can quadruple its revenue, that would likely result in the stock following suit, because it trades at a reasonable price tag of 28 times earnings.\nAlphabet is a bit trickier. I think that, with the investments it's making, it could grow revenue and profits at a 25% compound annual growth rate (CAGR), given that it grew at 24% in the second quarter. A 25% CAGR from now until the end of 2030 equates to a 158% growth rate.\nThat indicates that Alphabet's stock will more than double . So, a $1,000 investment split between these two stocks, using these projections, indicates a final value of over $3,000. That would be an incredible gain in just a bit over four years, making these two stocks no-brainer buys right now.\nBy Keithen Drury – Sep 26, 2026 at 10:15PM EST\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":\"v2:e114\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA's guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The guarantee cap is a separate exposure; it does not state the purchase obligations or supply commitments being forecast.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e115\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA's days sales outstanding is extending to 60 days.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The time customers take to pay does not establish the amount NVIDIA has committed to suppliers.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e116\",\"fact\":\"Huang said NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The size of the supply shortfall supports continued efforts to secure capacity, making commitments above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e130\",\"fact\":\"Kress guided approximately 70% fiscal 2028 revenue growth, constrained by supply.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Supply-constrained growth supports NVIDIA maintaining large supply commitments, making an amount above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e205\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA expects about 70% fiscal 2028 revenue growth amid supply constraints.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The supply-constrained growth outlook supports continued capacity commitments, making an amount above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e235\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 growth guidance is limited by supply, not demand.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Strong demand exceeding available supply supports ongoing capacity commitments, making an amount above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e238\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA guides fiscal 2028 gross margin to 72%–73%.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The margin forecast describes profitability, not the size of purchase obligations or supply commitments.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e240\",\"fact\":\"Huang said NVIDIA would double chip sales next year.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"A doubling of chip sales would require substantially more supply, supporting commitments above $200 billion; the report gives no commitment amount.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e282\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA forecasts doubling chip volume in 2027.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Doubling chip volume implies a need for more supply, making sustained commitments above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e286\",\"fact\":\"Kress expects roughly 70% fiscal 2028 revenue growth, constrained by supply.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"A manufacturing-constrained outlook supports continued supply commitments, making an amount above $200 billion more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e327\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA is expected to grow revenue 70% in 2027.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Expected growth supports demand for production capacity, making large commitments more likely; the expectation has no named source.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e339\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA estimates fiscal 2027 revenue growth of around 70%.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Growth in the fiscal year being assessed suggests demand for supply, making large commitments more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e351\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA estimates fiscal 2027 revenue growth of around 70%.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Growth in the fiscal year being assessed suggests demand for supply, making large commitments more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e366\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA management expects 70% revenue growth in 2027.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Expected growth suggests continued demand for supply and capacity, making large commitments more likely.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v2:e368\",\"fact\":\"Micron's new memory capacity will come online from mid-2027 into 2028.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Capacity arriving after NVIDIA's fiscal year-end supports continued memory scarcity, making large supply commitments more likely to persist.\"}"}}
